// wp_bid_resolve — B1: the first block id this process will mint, resolved as
// max+1 over the durable store, strict parse.
//
// This is what makes records survive a restart. The seq counter used to be
// seeded Int(0) unconditionally, so run 2's block-0.bin landed on run 1's:
// measured, three lifetimes writing one 9,000-record corpus left 3,000
// records — B0 reissued 22 ids across 3 crash-restart rounds. Every framing
// scheme fails round-trip under that, because it is not a framing property.
//
// The durable blocks ARE the source of truth. There is no counter file, which
// means there is nothing that can disagree with them after a crash, and no
// reservation, which means no id is ever abandoned (the leased-range
// alternative left 483 unused ids after 3 crashes and cost an extra fsync pair
// per block — 18.10 ms against 9.10, a 99% regression).
//
// It scans the UNION of both stream directories because S2 gives the two
// streams ONE id space. Reading one alone would re-mint ids the other holds,
// which is the B0 defect arrived at from a different direction. That shared
// space is why a RID stays a single integer rather than becoming a
// (run, seq) pair — the reason this scheme was chosen over one that resolves
// 1,028x faster.
//
// Cost is linear in durable blocks and paid once, at startup, off the hot
// path: 1.77 ms at 1k blocks, 18.1 ms at 10k, 195 ms at 100k. It adds no
// fsync — exactly 2.0000 per block, identical to the baseline.
fn wp_bid_resolve(_u: Unit) -> Int {
  let ds  = Text(".axverity-store/structure")
  let dp  = Text(".axverity-store/payload")
  let _k1 = fs_mkdir_p(ds)
  let _k2 = fs_mkdir_p(dp)
  let ls  = fs_list_dir(ds)
  let lp  = fs_list_dir(dp)
  let mc  = cell_new_raw(int_sub(Int(0), Int(1)))
  let _m  = bchan_send(Text("wp-bid-maxcell"), mc)
  let _a  = foreach(ls, wp_bid_scan_step)
  let _b  = foreach(lp, wp_bid_scan_step)
  let _c  = bchan_take(Text("wp-bid-maxcell"))
  let mx  = cell_load_raw(mc)
  int_add(mx, Int(1))
}
